      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "This is a long section with a lively story which introduces several important items of grammar, the dative case (GE pp. 176–180, #189–190), the aorist infinitive (GE pp. 187–189, #195–197), the aorist imperative (GE pp. 189–191, #198–200), γιγνώσκω (GE p. 201, #209), and the principal parts of common and very irregular verbs (GE p. 202, #211). None of this is difficult, but it pays to learn the new grammar carefully after each section in which it is introduced.",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": 1,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 2,
      "bbox": [
        215,
        215,
        461,
        908
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "Before you start you may like to look at the table (GE pp. 176–177, #103) where the forms of the dative are set out. Notice the predominance of ι:\nin -α -η -ω -ι -ει in the singular\nin -αις -οις -σι in the plural\nThe various uses of the dative are well described in GE pp. 179–180, #190.",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": 2,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
